      "text": "To find the largest integer that always divides the expression \\((10x+2)(10x+6)(5x+5)\\) for any odd \\(x\\), we will first simplify the expression and then analyze its divisibility properties.\n\n1. **Simplify the Expression**:\n    \\[\n    (10x+2)(10x+6)(5x+5)\n    \\]\n2. **Distribute each term**:\n    \n    \\[\n    (10x+2)(10x+6)(5x+5) = (10x+2)(10x+6)(5x+5)\n    \\]\n\nMultiply the factors:\n    \\[\n    = 10x(10x+6)(5x+5) + 2(10x+6)(5x+5) + 2(10x+6)(5x+5)\n    \\]\n    \n3. **Group terms**:\n    \\[\n    = 10x(10x+6)(5x+5) + (2(10x+6)(5x+5) + 2(10x+6)(5x+5))\n    \\]\n\nSimplify further:\n    \\[\n    = 10x(10x+6)(5x+5) + 2(10x+6)(5x+5)\n    \\]\n\nFactor out the common factor \\((10x+6)\\):\n    \\[\n    = (10x+6)(10x(5x+5) + 2(5x+5))\n    \\]\n\nSimplify the content under the parentheses:\n    \\[\n        = (10x+6)[10x(5x+5) + 2(5x+5)]\n        = (10x+6)[10x \\cdot 5x + 10x \\cdot 5 + 2 \\cdot 5x + 2 \\cdot 5]\n    \\]\n\n    \\[\n    = (10x+6)(50x^2 + 50x + 10x + 10)\n        = (10x+6)(50x^2 + 60x + 10)\n    \\]\n\nFactor common parts:\n    \\[\n    = (10x+6)(50x^2 + 60x + 10)\n    \\]\n\n    Further:\n    \\[\n    = 2(5x+3)(5x+1)(5x+5)\n    \\]\n\n\\[\nNote that \\(5x+1\\) will always be odd when \\(x\\) is an odd integer\n\n4. **Evaluate Divisibility**:\n    Since \\( (10x+2)(10x+6) = 2 * (5x+1)(5x+5) \\), the expression essentially consists of two identical pairings: 2 and (5x+1)(5x+5).\n\nHence, if \\(x\\) is an odd number, it contributes:\n- 2 as a factor,\n- (5x + 1 and 5x + 5) which both contribute factors of 5.\n\nTherefore, \\( (10x+2)(10x+6)(5x+5) \\) is divisible by \\(2*5*5 = 50\\):\n\nThus, the largest integer that always divides this expression is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{50}\n\\]",
