      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Given that \\(x\\) is an odd number, we need to find the largest integer that always divides the expression \\((10x+2)(10x+6)(5x+5)\\).\n\nFirst, let's factorize each term in the expression:\n\\[\n10x + 2 = 2(5x + 1), \\quad 10x + 6 = 2(5x + 3), \\quad 5x + 5 = 5(x + 1).\n\\]\nThus, the expression becomes:\n\\[\n(10x+2)(10x+6)(5x+5) = 2(5x+1) \\cdot 2(5x+3) \\cdot 5(x + 1) = 20(5x+1)(5x+3)(x+1).\n\\]\nSince both \\(5x+1\\) and \\(5x+3\\) are consecutive odd numbers, the product \\((5x+1)(5x+3)\\) is always divisible by 7 (the product of any two consecutive integers is always divisible by 2, and \\(5x+1\\) and \\(5x+3\\) differ by 2, hence, both are odd, making their product even, and divisible by 2. We also need to check the divisibility by 7. Since \\(5x+1\\) and \\(5x+3\\) are consecutive odd numbers, their difference is 2, and for any integer \\(x\\) that makes \\(5x+1\\) or \\(5x+3\\) a multiple of 7, the product will be divisible by 7. Therefore, the expression is always divisible by \\(2^2 \\times 7 = 28\\). There is no greater common factor that is guaranteed to divide any term, so the largest integer that always divides the expression is 28.\n\nA visual check confirms the solution.\nThe largest integer that always divides \\((10x+2)(10x+6)(5x+5)\\) is \\boxed{28}.",
