      "text": "   - First, factor the expression into prime factors and identify common terms that appear in each factorization.\n   - Recognize that $10x + 2 = 2(5x+1)$ and $10x + 6 = 2(5x+3)$, so the entire expression can be rewritten as $2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 5x + 1 \\cdot 5x + 3 = 2^2 \\cdot 5 \\cdot x + 5x + 6 = 5(2x+1)(5x+3)$.\n   - Observe that for odd $x$, the factor $2x+1$ appears and together with the factor $5x+3$ produces the specific pattern $5^3 \\cdot (2x+1)(5x+3)$, indicating that $5^3 = 125$ divides the original expression.\n   - Since any integer raised to the power of 1 results in itself, $5^1 = 5$, and therefore, the greatest common divisor of all possible odd values of $x$ is 125. Thus, the largest integer that always divides the expression is 125.",
